  "jobTitle": "Lead Software Engineer",
  "summary": "I am Eric McGinnis, a Baldwin Wallace graduate currently working as the Lead Software Engineer at Integrity Energy.  \n\nSince starting at Integrity Energy my responsibilities have increased outside of just development.  Being the only in-house developer and really the only in-house IT resource, I have had to expand our technology and our team to support our growing business.  I have since hired multiple other developers and have worked with off-shore and domestic consultants to increase throughput and create more valuable products.  We are continuing the search to grow our local team as well. \n\nAs a software developer for an independent energy broker, I have been exposed to the entire software stack.  From database to front-end, my day-to-day responsibilities include creating, modifying, and maintaining many internal and external facing web sites, services, and applications.  As well as working in the full stack I have had the opportunity to work with supporting technologies and tools, such as version control, continuous integration and deployment, application monitoring/scheduling, and a custom design system.\n\nI have experience both working with on-premises infrastructure, as well as cloud platforms such as AWS and Azure.  Application and solution infrastructure is my newest area of interest.  As we create new and continue to move existing projects to the cloud, we are continuously learning new and more efficient ways to doing things.\n\nI am always looking to expand my skill-set.  I very much enjoy working within the full stack and leveraging new technologies and techniques to stay on top of the industry.  I also enjoy being a part of the entire software development life cycle, including planning and design.  Every day I am looking to improve myself and lend my opinions and experiences in order to create better software and processes.  The most rewarding part of my job is collaborating with others to create innovative solutions to both simple and complex problems.",
